---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout title="Student walkthroughs" description="Step-by-step instructions for editing a portfolio, using Git and GitHub, and publishing the site.">
<div class="container student-guide">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">MIS CLUB · WORKSHOP 01 · STUDENT WALKTHROUGH</div>
    <h1>Build your resume site.<br/><span class="hero-accent">Publish it with GitHub Pages.</span></h1>
    <p>Set up the starter, fill it with your real details, practice a pull request, and publish the site. Each step includes commands and help for common errors.</p>
    <div class="guide-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="#step-1">Start with the setup <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="btn" href={base+'designs/'}>Preview & download starter ↓</a></div>
  </section>
  <section class="mission-map" aria-label="Portfolio setup steps">
    <div class="mission-map-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">WORKSHOP STEPS</div><h2>Use these steps to set up, edit, and publish your portfolio.</h2></div><p>Complete the steps in order, or skip ahead if your project already runs.</p></div>
    <div class="route-track" aria-label="Portfolio setup steps">
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-1" data-route-step="1"><span class="route-node">01</span><b>Tools</b><small>Check setup</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-2" data-route-step="2"><span class="route-node">02</span><b>Create a repo</b><small>Set up GitHub</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-3" data-route-step="3"><span class="route-node">03</span><b>Run locally</b><small>Start the site</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-4" data-route-step="4"><span class="route-node">04</span><b>Edit</b><small>Update the content</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-5" data-route-step="5"><span class="route-node">05</span><b>Review</b><small>Open a pull request</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-6" data-route-step="6"><span class="route-node">06</span><b>Publish</b><small>Check the live URL</small></a>
    </div>
    <div class="route-rescue">
      <div><b>Need help with a step?</b><span>Choose what’s happening to find the next instructions.</span></div>
      <div class="rescue-options" role="group" aria-label="Choose your current situation">
        <button type="button" data-route-help="start">Starting fresh</button>
        <button type="button" data-route-help="local">Local site works</button>
        <button type="button" data-route-help="push">Push failed</button>
        <button type="button" data-route-help="actions">Deploy failed</button>
      </div>
      <p id="route-advice" aria-live="polite">Choose the problem that best matches what you see.</p>
      <a id="route-next-link" href="#step-1">Go to step 1 →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="guide-status card">
    <div><div class="eyebrow">Progress tracker</div><h2 id="guide-progress-label">0 of 6 steps complete</h2><p class="muted small">Your checkpoints stay on this device. Nothing is sent to a server.</p></div>
    <div class="guide-progress"><div class="progress-wrap"><div class="progress-bar" id="guide-progress-bar"></div></div><span id="guide-progress-percent">0%</span></div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-1" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-number">01</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 01 · ACCESS + TOOLS</div><h2>Check your tools and accounts.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">Install anything missing now. You do not need prior VS Code experience — this guide names every click.</p>
      <div class="tool-cards tool-cards-five">
        <article><b>GitHub</b><p>Repository hosting and collaboration in your browser.</p><a href="https://github.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open GitHub ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>Git</b><p>Local version history: commits, branches, and diffs.</p><a href="https://git-scm.com/downloads" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Install Git ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>VS Code</b><p>The editor where you change files and review diffs.</p><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Install VS Code ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>Node.js + npm</b><p>Runs the Astro starter and installs its packages. Prefer Node.js 22 (what GitHub Actions uses). Node 18+ usually works for local preview.</p><a href="https://nodejs.org/en/download" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Install Node.js ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>Copilot</b><p>Optional assistant for explanations and focused code changes.</p><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/setup" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Copilot setup ↗</a></article>
      </div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>VS Code map (learn these three areas)</b><p><strong>Activity Bar</strong> — tall icon strip on the far left. Top icons: Explorer (files), Search, Source Control (Git branch icon), Extensions. <strong>Side Bar</strong> — panel that opens next to it (file tree, or Git changes). <strong>Editor</strong> — big center area where file text appears. <strong>Status Bar</strong> — thin strip at the very bottom (shows branch name later).</p></div>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · OPEN VS CODE ONCE</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Launch <strong>Visual Studio Code</strong> from your applications / Start menu (not a browser tab).</li>
          <li>If you see a welcome page, that is fine — leave it open for now.</li>
          <li>Click the top icon in the Activity Bar (files / two pages). That is <strong>Explorer</strong>.</li>
          <li>Open the menu <strong>Terminal → New Terminal</strong>. A panel opens at the bottom. That is where you type commands.</li>
          <li>Click inside the terminal panel so the cursor blinks, then paste or type the three version checks below and press Enter after each line.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-check">
        <div><span class="task-label">QUICK CHECK · TERMINAL IN VS CODE</span><pre id="tool-check-commands">git --version
node --version
npm --version</pre><button class="copy-button" id="copy-tool-check" type="button">Copy commands</button></div>
        <p><b>What you should see</b><br/>Three version numbers. Prefer <code>v22.x.x</code> for Node to match Actions; <code>v18+</code> is usually fine locally. If a command says “not found,” use the install links above, quit VS Code completely, reopen it, and try again.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Useful words</b><p><strong>Repository (repo):</strong> project files plus history. <strong>Commit:</strong> a saved checkpoint. <strong>Branch:</strong> a named line of work. <strong>Pull request (PR):</strong> a review request before merging into <code>main</code>. <strong>Publish:</strong> make the site available at a web address.</p></div>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · ACCOUNTS</span><ol><li>In a browser, sign in at <a href="https://github.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">github.com ↗</a>.</li><li>In VS Code, click the account / person icon near the bottom of the Activity Bar (or <strong>Accounts</strong> in the gear menu) and sign in with the <strong>same</strong> GitHub account if prompted.</li><li>Copilot is optional — if it is not ready, keep going without AI.</li></ol></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="1"/> I can open GitHub, open VS Code, and see version numbers in the terminal.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-2" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-number">02</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 02 · CREATE A REPOSITORY</div><h2>Create your repository.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">You’ll create your own copy of the starter so you can change it and publish it from your GitHub account.</p>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · NEW REPOSITORY</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/creating-and-managing-repositories/quickstart-for-repositories" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/repository/create-repository-name.png" alt="Screenshot from GitHub Docs showing the real new-repository form and Repository name field." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-create-repository.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real GitHub UI from the official docs. Open <a href="https://github.com/new" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub’s New repository page ↗</a>; the fields may differ slightly from this documentation screenshot.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · DOWNLOAD + OPEN IN VS CODE</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Open the <a href={base+'designs/'}>Portfolio lab</a> and click <strong>Download starter ZIP ↓</strong>.</li>
          <li>Find the ZIP in your Downloads folder (name includes <code>portfolio-starter</code>). Double-click it to extract. You should get a folder named <code>my-resume-site</code>.</li>
          <li>In VS Code: menu <strong>File → Open Folder…</strong> (Mac: <strong>File → Open…</strong> and choose the folder). Select the <code>my-resume-site</code> folder itself — not your Downloads folder, and not a single file inside.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Open</strong> / <strong>Select Folder</strong>. If VS Code asks “Do you trust the authors of the files in this folder?”, choose <strong>Yes, I trust the authors</strong>.</li>
          <li>Click the Explorer icon (top of the Activity Bar). In the file list you should see <code>package.json</code>, <code>src</code>, <code>public</code>, and <code>WORKSHOP.md</code>. If you only see one nested folder, you opened one level too high — use <strong>File → Open Folder</strong> again on the inner <code>my-resume-site</code>.</li>
          <li>Optional: open <code>WORKSHOP.md</code> beside your work as an in-editor checklist. Stock Markdown Preview boxes are not clickable — mark steps done by editing <code>[ ]</code> → <code>[x]</code> in the file. Same journey as this page (this guide has more screenshots and copy examples).</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · CREATE AN EMPTY GITHUB REPO</span>
        <ol>
          <li>In your browser, open <a href="https://github.com/new" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">github.com/new ↗</a>.</li>
          <li>Repository name: something simple like <code>my-resume-site</code>.</li>
          <li>Choose <strong>Public</strong>.</li>
          <li>Leave <strong>Add a README</strong>, license, and .gitignore <strong>unchecked</strong>. The starter already has files; adding those now causes merge conflicts later.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Create repository</strong>.</li>
          <li>On the empty repo page, copy the HTTPS URL (looks like <code>https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY.git</code>). Paste it into a notes app for Step 3.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">Do not push yet. First confirm the site runs on your computer in Step 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Why Public?</b><p>GitHub Pages for a normal project repository is free when the repository is <strong>Public</strong>. A Private repository usually cannot publish a free Pages site on a free student account.</p></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Why this matters</b><p>Your repository will store the site and its history. You change it, review it, and publish it from your own GitHub account.</p></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="2"/> I downloaded the starter ZIP and created an empty public GitHub repository.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-3" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-number">03</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 03 · RUN THE PROJECT</div><h2>Run the project locally.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">GitHub hosts the shared copy. In VS Code, you’ll open your local copy and run it before editing.</p>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Git in one sentence</b><p><strong>Commit</strong> = save a checkpoint on your laptop. <strong>Push</strong> = upload commits to GitHub. <strong>Branch</strong> = a named copy of the work so you can review before it lands on <code>main</code>. You will use the terminal for the first push, then VS Code buttons for the pull request later.</p></div>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · PREVIEW LOCALLY</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Confirm Explorer still shows <code>package.json</code> and <code>src</code> at the top level of the open folder.</li>
          <li>Menu <strong>Terminal → New Terminal</strong>. Click inside the bottom panel so you can type.</li>
          <li>Type or paste <code>npm install</code> and press Enter. Wait until it finishes (can take 1–2 minutes). Errors about network usually mean retry or ask the facilitator.</li>
          <li>Type or paste <code>npm run dev</code> and press Enter. Leave this terminal running.</li>
          <li>In the terminal output, find a line with <code>http://localhost:4321</code> (or similar). Hold <kbd>Cmd</kbd> (Mac) or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> (Windows) and click the link, or copy it into your browser.</li>
          <li>You should see the sample resume site. Keep that browser tab open.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">The local address works only on your computer — it is not the public website yet. If the terminal is “busy” with <code>npm run dev</code>, open a <strong>second</strong> terminal with the <strong>+</strong> button in the terminal panel title bar before running Git commands.</p>
      </div>
<div class="command-grid command-grid-short">
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>Install project tools</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-npm-install">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-npm-install">npm install</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>Start the local preview</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-npm-dev">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-npm-dev">npm run dev</pre></div>
</div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Ask Copilot to explain the project</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="explain-project">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="explain-project">Explain this project in plain English. Tell me which file creates the page and where I should edit my name, education, coursework, experience, skills, and projects in profile.js. Do not change any files yet. Use short sentences.</pre></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Read this before editing</b><p>It is okay if the code looks unfamiliar. First learn which file does what. Ask Copilot to explain; do not ask it to rewrite the entire project.</p></div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · PUSH TO GITHUB</span>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Keep the preview running if you can:</strong> in the terminal panel title bar, click <strong>+</strong> to open a second terminal. Run the Git commands there. (If you only have one terminal, stop the preview with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> — on a Mac that is still Control+C, not Command+C — then run Git, then start <code>npm run dev</code> again later.)</li>
          <li>Click inside the Git terminal so the cursor blinks. Confirm the prompt is inside your <code>my-resume-site</code> folder (you should see that name in the path).</li>
          <li>Run the commands below one at a time. Replace the uppercase placeholders with your GitHub username and repository name from Step 2.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">Copy one command at a time. Use the HTTPS URL from your empty repository page.</p>
      </div>
<div class="command-grid">
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>1 · Start Git history</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-init">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-init">git init</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>2 · Stage the project files</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-add">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-add">git add .</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>3 · Inspect what is staged</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-first-diff">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-first-diff">git status
git diff --staged</pre><p class="help-text">Check for passwords, tokens, private files, or anything you do not want to publish.</p></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>4 · Make the first commit</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-commit">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-commit">git commit -m "Create my portfolio"</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>5 · Name the main branch</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-branch">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-branch">git branch -M main</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>6 · Connect your GitHub repository</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-remote">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-remote">git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY.git</pre><p class="help-text">Change both capitalized placeholders first.</p></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>7 · Send your files to GitHub</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-push">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-push">git push -u origin main</pre></div>
</div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Expected: a red X in Actions</b><p>Right after this push, open the <strong>Actions</strong> tab. The workflow often fails on <strong>deploy</strong> with a message about enabling GitHub Pages. That is normal. <strong>Do not rewrite files to “fix” it yet.</strong> Finish personalizing and the pull request in Steps 4–5, then turn Pages on and Re-run in Step 6.</p></div>
      <details class="terminal-details">
        <summary>Git says “Author identity unknown”?</summary>
        <p>Git stores a name and email with each commit. These identify the commit author; they are not your GitHub password. For a public portfolio, consider using the GitHub noreply address shown in <b>GitHub → Settings → Emails</b>.</p>
        <div class="command-grid command-grid-short">
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Set your commit name</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-user-name">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-user-name">git config --global user.name "Your Name"</pre></div>
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Set your commit email</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-user-email">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-user-email">git config --global user.email "YOUR-GITHUB-NOREPLY-EMAIL"</pre><p class="help-text">Replace the placeholder with your GitHub noreply email, then retry the commit.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="terminal-details">
        <summary>Push asks for a password / authentication failed?</summary>
        <p>GitHub no longer accepts your normal account password for <code>git push</code> over HTTPS. Use one of these options:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Browser sign-in (easiest on many laptops):</strong> when VS Code or Git opens a browser window, sign in and approve access, then retry <code>git push -u origin main</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Personal Access Token:</strong> on GitHub go to <strong>Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens</strong>, create a classic token with the <code>repo</code> scope, then paste the token when the terminal asks for a password (username = your GitHub username).</li>
          <li><strong>Ask the facilitator</strong> if you are stuck for more than a few minutes — do not force-push.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">Official guide: <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/creating-a-personal-access-token" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Creating a personal access token ↗</a></p>
      </details>
      <details class="terminal-details">
        <summary>Command fails: remote origin already exists?</summary>
        <p>Run <code>git remote -v</code>. If it already points at your empty repo URL, skip <code>git remote add</code> and go straight to <code>git push -u origin main</code>. If it points somewhere else, ask the facilitator before changing it.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="terminal-details">
        <summary>I committed on main by accident before the pull request?</summary>
        <p>Stop and tell a facilitator. Show them your Status Bar branch name and the output of <code>git status</code>. Do not run random “fix” commands from the internet.</p>
      </details>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="3"/> I previewed the site locally and pushed it to my GitHub repository.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-4" data-step="4">
    <div class="step-number">04</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 04 · BUILD YOUR RESUME</div><h2>Replace the sample content with yours.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">This is the main work of the workshop. You do <strong>not</strong> need to know JavaScript, HTML, or CSS. You will open one file (<code>src/data/profile.js</code>), change the sample text to your information, and save. The website reads that file and updates. Only publish details you are comfortable making public.</p>
      <div class="starter-callout">
        <div>
          <strong>One starter for everyone</strong>
          <p>Empty sections stay hidden. Fill coursework and projects now; add experience when you have it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="design-download">
          <a href={base+'previews/resume/'}>Preview ↗</a>
          <a href={base+'downloads/portfolio-starter.zip'}>Download ZIP ↓</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="explorer-figure" aria-label="Where to find profile.js in VS Code">
        <div class="screen-label"><span>VS CODE · EXPLORER</span><span>FIND THIS FILE</span></div>
        <div class="explorer-panel">
          <div class="explorer-sidebar" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="explorer-title">EXPLORER</div>
            <ul class="explorer-tree">
              <li class="folder open">my-resume-site</li>
              <li class="indent folder open">public</li>
              <li class="indent2 file">photo.svg</li>
              <li class="indent2 file muted">PHOTO-README.txt</li>
              <li class="indent folder open">src</li>
              <li class="indent2 folder open">data</li>
              <li class="indent3 file active">profile.js</li>
              <li class="indent2 folder">layouts</li>
              <li class="indent2 folder">pages</li>
              <li class="indent file">package.json</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="explorer-copy">
            <p class="eyebrow">PATH TO OPEN</p>
            <p class="explorer-path"><code>src</code> → <code>data</code> → <code>profile.js</code></p>
            <p>In the left sidebar (Explorer), expand <strong>src</strong>, then <strong>data</strong>, then click <strong>profile.js</strong>. That single file holds your name, education, coursework, experience, projects, and photo setting.</p>
            <p class="help-text">Optional photo files go in <code>public/</code> (for example <code>public/photo.jpg</code>), not inside <code>src/</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="plain-note"><b>Do not commit yet</b><p>Edit and save locally in this step. Stay on <code>main</code> if you want, but <strong>do not Commit or Sync in Source Control yet</strong>. In Step 5 you will create a branch, then commit the reviewed change there. Committing personalization on <code>main</code> now skips the pull-request practice.</p></div>

      <div class="code-decoder" aria-label="How to read profile.js without knowing JavaScript">
        <div class="code-decoder-head">
          <span class="eyebrow">NO CODING BACKGROUND NEEDED</span>
          <h3>How to read <code>profile.js</code> (cheat sheet)</h3>
          <p>This file is just labeled boxes of text. You change the words inside quotes. Hover a chip for a short tip, or read the line under it.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="symbol-tips">
          <li>
            <button type="button" class="symbol-chip" aria-describedby="tip-quotes"><code>'text'</code></button>
            <span id="tip-quotes" class="symbol-tip" role="tooltip"><b>Quotes</b> — your real words go between the single quotes. Change <code>'Jordan Lee'</code> to <code>'Your Name'</code>. Leave the quotes in place.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <button type="button" class="symbol-chip" aria-describedby="tip-label"><code>name:</code></button>
            <span id="tip-label" class="symbol-tip" role="tooltip"><b>Label</b> — the word before the colon names the field. Do not rename <code>name:</code>, <code>skills:</code>, or <code>education:</code>. Only change the value after it.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <button type="button" class="symbol-chip" aria-describedby="tip-comma"><code>,</code></button>
            <span id="tip-comma" class="symbol-tip" role="tooltip"><b>Comma</b> — separates items in a list (like a list separator in a spreadsheet). Missing commas are the #1 cause of red errors after editing.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <button type="button" class="symbol-chip" aria-describedby="tip-brackets"><code>[ ... ]</code></button>
            <span id="tip-brackets" class="symbol-tip" role="tooltip"><b>Square brackets</b> — a list that can hold one item or many. Everything you add for skills/schools/projects stays inside matching <code>[</code> and <code>]</code>.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <button type="button" class="symbol-chip" aria-describedby="tip-braces"><code>{'{'} ... {'}'}</code></button>
            <span id="tip-braces" class="symbol-tip" role="tooltip"><b>Curly braces</b> — one “card” or entry (one school, one job, one project). Several cards sit inside one list.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <button type="button" class="symbol-chip" aria-describedby="tip-empty"><code>[]</code></button>
            <span id="tip-empty" class="symbol-tip" role="tooltip"><b>Empty list</b> — means “nothing to show.” The website hides that section. Fine for experience if you have no job yet.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <details class="copy-coach">
          <summary>What does the yellow <b>Copy</b> button actually do?</summary>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Copy</strong> puts an example onto your clipboard (like Copy in a Word doc). It does <em>not</em> change your website by itself.</li>
            <li>Click into <code>profile.js</code> in VS Code where the instructions say (usually inside a <code>[ ... ]</code> list).</li>
            <li><strong>Paste</strong> with <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd>.</li>
            <li>Replace the example words with <em>your</em> information. Save. Check localhost.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="help-text">Prefer editing the sample text already in the file when you can. Use Copy only when you need an extra skill, school, or project.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · OPEN THE CONTENT FILE</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Click the Explorer icon (top of the Activity Bar) so the file tree is visible.</li>
          <li>Click the arrow next to <code>src</code> to expand it, then <code>data</code>, then click <code>profile.js</code>. The file opens in the center editor.</li>
          <li>Near the top of the file, find lines like <code>name: 'Jordan Lee',</code>. Change only the words <strong>between the quotes</strong> for <code>name</code>, <code>role</code>, <code>location</code>, <code>tagline</code>, <code>about</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>github</code>, and <code>linkedin</code> (no leftover <code>example.com</code> links).</li>
          <li>Save with <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd> (Mac) or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd> (Windows). A white dot on the tab means the file is unsaved — save until the dot disappears.</li>
          <li>Keep the local preview open (<code>npm run dev</code>). After each save, the browser usually updates on its own; if not, refresh the localhost tab.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">Leave any list as <code>[]</code> if you do not have that content yet — empty sections do not appear on the page.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="plain-note"><b>Lists support multiple entries — that is the point</b>
        <p>In <code>profile.js</code>, anything written as <code>something: [ ... ]</code> is a <strong>list</strong>. One item inside the brackets = one row/card on the site. Two items = two rows/cards. Put as many as you need for skills, schools, classes, jobs, and projects.</p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> a real resume rarely has one skill or one school. Multiple entries let the page show your full story without editing the website layout.</p>
        <p><strong>How (same pattern every time):</strong> keep items inside the matching <code>[ ... ]</code>. Separate items with commas. Add another by pasting/typing a new item after the previous one’s comma. Delete an item by removing that whole chunk. Do not create a second <code>skills:</code> or <code>education:</code> label — only add items inside the existing list.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · SKILLS (MULTIPLE TAGS)</span>
        <ol>
          <li>In <code>profile.js</code>, press <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> and search for <code>skills:</code>.</li>
          <li>You should see several quoted strings inside square brackets — that is already a multi-item list. Each string becomes one skill chip on the page.</li>
          <li><strong>Why more than one:</strong> employers scan for several tools/topics. Aim for a short honest set (often 5–10), not a single skill.</li>
          <li>Replace the samples with yours. Keep each skill in quotes, with a comma after each line (a trailing comma after the last skill is fine).</li>
          <li><strong>Add another skill:</strong> after any skill’s comma, press Enter and type a new line like <code>'Threat modeling',</code>. Repeat for as many as you want.</li>
          <li><strong>Remove a skill:</strong> delete that whole line (quotes + comma). Save and check the Skills section on localhost.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">Skills are plain text tags (not objects). Wrong: <code>{'{'} name: 'Python' {'}'}</code>. Right: <code>'Python',</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card has-coach">
        <div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · SKILLS LIST (SEVERAL ENTRIES)</span><h3>Example list — then change the words to yours</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="skills-list" data-copy-reset="Copy example">Copy example</button></div>
        <ol class="coach-steps">
          <li>In <code>profile.js</code>, select your whole <code>skills: [ ... ],</code> block (from <code>skills:</code> through the closing <code>],</code>).</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Copy example</strong>, then paste over the selection (or edit the existing lines by hand — safer if you only need small changes).</li>
          <li>Change each <code>'...'</code> to a skill you actually have. Add more quoted lines for more skills.</li>
        </ol>
        <pre id="skills-list">skills: [
  'Python',
  'SQL',
  'Git & GitHub',
  'Technical writing',
  'Networking basics',
],</pre>
        <p class="help-text">Each quoted line is one skill chip on the page. You are editing text, not writing a program.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · EDUCATION (ONE OR MORE SCHOOLS)</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Search for <code>education:</code>. That starts a list of school objects inside <code>[ ... ]</code>.</li>
          <li>The starter usually has <strong>one</strong> school object. Edit the quoted fields:
            <ul>
              <li><code>school</code> — college or university name</li>
              <li><code>degree</code> — what you are earning or earned</li>
              <li><code>detail</code> — years or status (for example <code>Expected graduation · 2027</code>)</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li><strong>Why more than one:</strong> list every school you want on the resume (community college + WSU, bachelor’s + master’s, dual enrollment, etc.). Each object is a separate Education row on the site.</li>
          <li><strong>Add a second (or third) school:</strong> copy the object below → paste it <strong>inside</strong> <code>education: [ ... ]</code> after the previous object’s closing <code>{'}'},</code> → change the text. Order is newest/most relevant first if you like.</li>
          <li>Save and confirm every school appears under Education on localhost.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card has-coach">
        <div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · TWO SCHOOLS</span><h3>Full <code>education</code> example with two schools</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="education-list" data-copy-reset="Copy example">Copy example</button></div>
        <ol class="coach-steps">
          <li><strong>Why two blocks?</strong> Each <code>{'{'} ... {'}'}</code> is one school on the page. This example shows WSU + a prior college.</li>
          <li>Select your existing <code>education: [ ... ],</code> block in <code>profile.js</code>.</li>
          <li>Copy example → paste over it → change every quoted value to your schools (or delete the second school if you only have one).</li>
        </ol>
        <pre id="education-list">education: [
  {'{'}
    school: 'Wichita State University',
    degree: 'B.B.A. Management Information Systems',
    detail: 'Expected graduation · 2027',
  {'}'},
  {'{'}
    school: 'Cowley College',
    degree: 'A.A. Information Assurance · A.S. Computer Science',
    detail: 'Completed · 2014',
  {'}'},
],</pre>
        <p class="help-text">Notice the comma after the first school’s closing <code>{'}'}</code> — that comma separates school #1 from school #2.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card has-coach">
        <div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · ONE MORE SCHOOL</span><h3>Add a school without replacing the whole list</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="education-object" data-copy-reset="Copy example">Copy example</button></div>
        <ol class="coach-steps">
          <li>Find your last school’s closing <code>{'}'},</code> inside <code>education: [ ... ]</code>.</li>
          <li>Click after that comma → Copy example → paste → edit the quoted text.</li>
          <li>Stay inside the same <code>[ ... ]</code>. Do not paste this at the top of the file.</li>
        </ol>
        <pre id="education-object">{'{'}
  school: 'Friends University',
  degree: 'M.S. Cyber Security',
  detail: 'In progress',
{'}'},</pre>
      </div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · COURSEWORK &amp; ACTIVITIES (MULTIPLE ITEMS)</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Search for <code>coursework:</code> — a list of class objects inside <code>[ ... ]</code>. The starter already has several; that is normal.</li>
          <li><strong>Why more than one:</strong> show the classes that support your story (security, databases, systems). Same for <code>activities:</code> — club + volunteer + meetup can all appear.</li>
          <li>Edit sample <code>title</code> / <code>detail</code> (and for activities, <code>organization</code>) to your real items, or delete objects you do not want.</li>
          <li><strong>Add another:</strong> copy the matching block below → paste <strong>inside</strong> that list after an existing <code>{'}'},</code> → change the text. Repeat for as many classes or activities as you want.</li>
          <li>You can leave <code>experience: []</code> empty until you have a job or internship to list.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">Clear a whole section with <code>coursework: [],</code> or <code>activities: [],</code> if you prefer not to show it — empty lists stay hidden on the site.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card has-coach">
        <div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · COURSEWORK OBJECT</span><h3>One more class card</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="coursework-object" data-copy-reset="Copy example">Copy example</button></div>
        <ol class="coach-steps">
          <li>Search <code>coursework:</code>. Paste this <strong>inside</strong> that list after an existing class’s <code>{'}'},</code>.</li>
          <li>Change <code>title</code> and <code>detail</code> (keep the quotes). Each object = one class on the site.</li>
        </ol>
        <pre id="coursework-object">{'{'}
  title: 'Database Management',
  detail: 'Relational modeling, SQL queries, and access control concepts.',
{'}'},</pre>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card has-coach">
        <div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · ACTIVITY OBJECT</span><h3>One more activity card</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="activity-object" data-copy-reset="Copy example">Copy example</button></div>
        <ol class="coach-steps">
          <li>Search <code>activities:</code>. Paste inside that list the same way as coursework.</li>
          <li>Fill <code>title</code>, <code>organization</code>, and <code>detail</code> with your real involvement.</li>
        </ol>
        <pre id="activity-object">{'{'}
  title: 'Cyber Club Member',
  organization: 'Campus technology club',
  detail: 'Weekly labs, capture-the-flag practice, and peer study sessions.',
{'}'},</pre>
      </div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · EXPERIENCE (ZERO, ONE, OR MANY JOBS)</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Search for <code>experience:</code>. Beginners often see <code>experience: [],</code> — that empty list means “no jobs shown yet,” which is fine.</li>
          <li><strong>Why multiple:</strong> each job/internship/volunteer role is its own object. Two jobs = two Experience entries on the site (newest first is typical).</li>
          <li>For your first role: replace <code>[]</code> with a list that contains one object, or paste the copy block below between <code>[</code> and <code>]</code>.</li>
          <li>Fill in <code>title</code>, <code>organization</code>, <code>date</code>, and <code>summary</code>. Optional: add a short <code>highlights</code> list of bullet strings.</li>
          <li><strong>Add another role:</strong> paste another object after the previous <code>{'}'},</code> (still inside the same <code>experience: [ ... ]</code>). Internships, campus jobs, volunteering, and class leadership all count.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card has-coach">
        <div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · EXPERIENCE OBJECT</span><h3>One job / internship card</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="experience-object" data-copy-reset="Copy example">Copy example</button></div>
        <ol class="coach-steps">
          <li>If you see <code>experience: [],</code>, replace the empty <code>[]</code> with <code>[</code> + this object + <code>]</code> (or paste the object between new brackets).</li>
          <li>If you already have one job, paste another object after its <code>{'}'},</code> for a second role.</li>
          <li>Change every quoted value. <code>highlights</code> is an optional mini-list of bullet strings inside the job card.</li>
        </ol>
        <pre id="experience-object">{'{'}
  title: 'Campus IT Helper',
  organization: 'University Help Desk',
  date: '2025–2026',
  summary: 'Answered student tech questions, reset accounts, and documented common fixes for the team.',
  highlights: [
    'Wrote a short FAQ the desk reused during busy weeks.',
  ],
{'}'},</pre>
      </div>

      <div class="plain-note"><b>What “projects” means here</b><p>The <code>projects</code> list in <code>profile.js</code> is the source of the <strong>Projects</strong> section on your resume site. <strong>Each object = one project card.</strong> The starter ships with three samples so you can see multiple entries already working — replace them with your real work, keep one and delete the rest, or add more objects for additional projects. If an object has a <code>slug</code>, the site also builds a detail page at <code>/projects/your-slug/</code>.</p></div>

      <figure class="explorer-figure" aria-label="Where the projects list sits inside profile.js">
        <div class="screen-label"><span>INSIDE profile.js · FIND THIS LIST</span><span>SEARCH: projects:</span></div>
        <div class="explorer-panel profile-anatomy">
