  <section class="guide-step" id="step-5" data-step="5">
    <div class="step-number">05</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 05 · REVIEW YOUR CHANGE</div><h2>Ship your resume work through a pull request.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">You’ve been editing locally. Now practice how teams ship: issue → branch → commit → pull request → partner +1 → merge. Primary path is VS Code Source Control + GitHub in the browser. Your personalization (and the featured project) can go in this PR.</p>
      <div class="mini-workflow"><span>Issue</span><b>→</b><span>Branch</span><b>→</b><span>Commit</span><b>→</b><span>Pull request</span><b>→</b><span>Merge</span></div>

      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · CREATE AN ISSUE</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/using-issues/creating-an-issue" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/issues/issue-title-body.png" alt="Real GitHub issue creation form with title and body fields filled in." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-issue.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real GitHub issue form. Write what should change and how you will prove it is done.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · 1 · CREATE AN ISSUE (GITHUB IN THE BROWSER)</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Open your repository on GitHub.</li>
          <li>Click the <strong>Issues</strong> tab, then <strong>New issue</strong>.</li>
          <li>If GitHub offers <strong>Featured project</strong>, choose it (from the starter’s issue template). Otherwise use the title and body below.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Create</strong>. Note the issue number (for example <code>#1</code>) — you will put <code>Closes #1</code> in the pull request.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · ISSUE</span><h3>Title + body (if the template does not appear)</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="issue-template">Copy</button></div><pre id="issue-template">Title: Add a featured project card

## What I’m adding or updating

Featured project card for: [your project name]

## Done when

- [ ] Project title and summary are honest (my real work)
- [ ] Link works (repo, demo, or doc) — not an example.com placeholder
- [ ] Looks readable on a phone-width browser window
- [ ] No secrets, passwords, or private personal details in the change</pre></div>

      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>VISUAL STUDIO CODE · SOURCE CONTROL</span><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/sourcecontrol/overview" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://code.visualstudio.com/assets/docs/sourcecontrol/overview/overview.png" alt="Real VS Code interface with the Source Control view and a side-by-side file diff." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/vscode-source-control.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real VS Code Source Control view. You will use this panel to create a branch, stage, commit, and publish.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · 2 · CREATE A BRANCH (VS CODE)</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Look at the <strong>Status Bar</strong> (very bottom of VS Code). You should see <code>main</code> (or similar) on the left.</li>
          <li>Click that branch name. A picker opens at the top of the window.</li>
          <li>Choose <strong>Create new branch…</strong> (wording may be <strong>Create new branch from…</strong>).</li>
          <li>Type <code>add-featured-project</code> and press Enter. Confirm you are branching from <code>main</code> if asked.</li>
          <li>Check the Status Bar again — it should now say <code>add-featured-project</code>. Do not edit until this is true.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">A branch is a separate line of work. Feature work stays off <code>main</code> until you review and merge.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · 3 · MAKE THE CHANGE</span>
        <ol>
          <li>In Explorer, open <code>src/data/profile.js</code> again (same file as Step 4).</li>
          <li>Search for <code>projects:</code>. Edit the featured project object so it matches your issue — real <code>title</code>, <code>summary</code>, and <code>link</code> (see Step 4 if you need the field-by-field walkthrough).</li>
          <li>Save the file (<kbd>Cmd</kbd>/<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>).</li>
          <li>Refresh the local preview. Check the Projects section on the home page and open the project detail page if you set a <code>slug</code>. Confirm desktop and a narrow window.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · 4 · STAGE, COMMIT, AND PUBLISH (VS CODE)</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Click the <strong>Source Control</strong> icon in the Activity Bar (branch / fork icon; often shows a number badge).</li>
          <li>Under <strong>Changes</strong>, find <code>profile.js</code>. Click the file name once to open the diff (green/red lines). Read it — only your intended edits should appear.</li>
          <li>Click the <strong>+</strong> next to the file (or next to <strong>Changes</strong>) to <strong>Stage</strong> it. The file moves under <strong>Staged Changes</strong>.</li>
          <li>In the message box at the top of Source Control, type a clear message such as <code>Add featured project card</code>.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Commit</strong> (checkmark). If VS Code offers to stage all files, only commit after you have staged the files you want.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Publish Branch</strong> or <strong>Sync Changes</strong> / cloud icon (wording varies). Approve the browser/GitHub sign-in if prompted. This uploads your branch.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">Staging selects what goes into the commit. Committing saves history on your computer. Publishing / syncing sends the branch to GitHub — that is when a pull request becomes possible.</p>
      </div>

      <section class="git-state-lab" aria-labelledby="git-state-title">
        <div class="lab-heading"><div><div class="eyebrow">WHAT JUST HAPPENED</div><h3 id="git-state-title">Edited → staged → committed → pushed</h3><p>Click each state to see what Git is tracking. Same ideas as the VS Code buttons above.</p></div><span class="lab-status">LOCAL → GITHUB</span></div>
        <div class="git-state-track" role="group" aria-label="Git change lifecycle">
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button is-current" data-git-state="0" aria-pressed="true"><span>01</span><b>Edited</b><small>Working tree</small></button>
          <span class="state-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button" data-git-state="1" aria-pressed="false"><span>02</span><b>Staged</b><small>Ready for commit</small></button>
          <span class="state-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button" data-git-state="2" aria-pressed="false"><span>03</span><b>Committed</b><small>Saved to history</small></button>
          <span class="state-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button" data-git-state="3" aria-pressed="false"><span>04</span><b>Pushed</b><small>Shared on GitHub</small></button>
        </div>
        <div class="git-state-detail">
          <div class="fake-terminal-real-meaning"><div class="terminal-chrome"><span>● ● ●</span><b>TERMINAL · EXAMPLE OUTPUT</b></div><pre id="git-state-output">On branch add-featured-project
Changes not staged for commit:
  modified: src/data/profile.js

no changes added to commit</pre></div>
          <div class="git-state-explanation"><span class="state-number" id="git-state-number">STATE 01 / 04</span><h4 id="git-state-heading">The edit exists only on your computer.</h4><p id="git-state-copy">Git can see the change, but it is not selected for the next commit.</p><div class="next-command"><div><span>IN VS CODE</span><code id="git-state-command">Source Control → stage profile.js (+)</code></div><button class="copy-button" type="button" id="copy-git-state">Copy hint</button></div><p class="lab-small-note" id="git-state-note">Staging a file does not upload it to GitHub.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · REVIEW A PULL REQUEST</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/how-tos/review-pull-requests/reviewing-proposed-changes-in-a-pull-request" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pull_requests/diff-settings-menu.png" alt="Real GitHub pull request Files changed view and diff settings." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-pull-request.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real GitHub review interface. Open <b>Files changed</b>, read what changed, and compare the diff with your issue before you merge.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Attach the pull request to the issue</b>
        <p>An issue is the <em>task</em>. A pull request is the <em>proposed fix</em>. You must <strong>link (attach) them</strong> so GitHub connects the work.</p>
        <p><strong>Two ways (do at least one; both is fine):</strong></p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Keywords in the PR body (preferred today)</strong> — put <code>Closes #1</code> on its own line (your real number). That attaches the PR <em>and</em> auto-closes the issue when you merge.</li>
          <li><strong>Link an issue in the GitHub UI</strong> — on the PR page, right sidebar → <strong>Development</strong> → <strong>Link an issue</strong> (or gear / “+”) → pick your issue. That attaches them in the UI. Still add <code>Closes #…</code> if you want the issue to close automatically on merge.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>How to check it worked:</strong> open the <strong>issue</strong> page. Under <strong>Development</strong> you should see this PR. Open the <strong>PR</strong> page — under <strong>Development</strong> you should see the issue. If either is missing, use one of the two methods above.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · 5 · OPEN THE PULL REQUEST AND LINK THE ISSUE</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Refresh your repository on GitHub. Often a yellow banner appears: <strong>Compare &amp; pull request</strong> — click it. If not: <strong>Pull requests → New pull request</strong>.</li>
          <li>Confirm <strong>base:</strong> <code>main</code> and <strong>compare:</strong> <code>add-featured-project</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Method A — keywords:</strong> in the PR description (body), put <code>Closes #1</code> on its <strong>own line</strong> — replace <code>1</code> with <em>your</em> issue number. Add a short summary under it (copy block below).</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Create pull request</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Method B — UI (recommended check):</strong> on the PR page, look at the right sidebar for <strong>Development</strong>. Click <strong>Link an issue</strong> (wording may be a gear icon or <strong>+</strong>). Select the issue you created. Confirm the issue title appears under Development on the PR.</li>
          <li>Open the <strong>issue</strong> page and confirm this PR appears under <strong>Development</strong> / linked pull requests.</li>
          <li>Open the PR’s <strong>Files changed</strong> tab. Read the diff yourself. Does it match your issue?</li>
          <li><strong>Do not merge yet.</strong> Copy your PR URL and hand it to the person beside you.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">If you only use the UI link without <code>Closes #…</code>, the issue may stay open after merge. Keep <code>Closes #N</code> in the description so merge closes the issue.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · 5 · PARTNER +1 REVIEW</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Pick a partner next to you. Swap PR links (or open each other’s GitHub repo → <strong>Pull requests</strong>).</li>
          <li>On your partner’s PR: open <strong>Files changed</strong>. Skim the diff — does the change match their issue / look like their resume content?</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Review changes</strong> → choose <strong>Approve</strong> → leave a short note (for example <code>LGTM +1 — featured project looks good</code>) → <strong>Submit review</strong>. If Approve is unavailable, leave a <strong>Comment</strong> review with <code>+1</code> instead.</li>
          <li>Wait until you have a +1 on <em>your</em> PR. Then: <strong>Merge pull request</strong> → <strong>Confirm merge</strong>. Delete the branch if GitHub offers. Check that the issue shows <strong>Closed</strong>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">Odd number in the room? Form a trio and each person reviews one neighbor. Facilitator can +1 if someone is alone.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · PR DESCRIPTION</span><h3>Paste into the pull request body</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="pr-body">Copy</button></div><pre id="pr-body">Closes #1

## Summary
Ships the featured project card and my resume personalization from Workshop 1.

## Tested
- [ ] Localhost looks right on desktop and narrow width
- [ ] Project / profile links are mine (not example.com)</pre>
        <p class="help-text">Change <code>#1</code> to match your issue number.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Review your own change</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="review-change">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="review-change">Review my current changes against these acceptance checks: [paste your checks]. Look for broken links, mobile layout problems, accessibility issues, accidental secrets, and unrelated edits. Explain any problems in plain English. Do not make more changes until I review your findings.</pre></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Before you merge</b><p>You and a partner both read <strong>Files changed</strong>. Merge only after you have a partner +1 (Approve or Comment). Do not merge only because a button is green or Copilot says it is finished.</p></div>

      <details class="terminal-details"><summary>Optional: same steps in the terminal</summary>
        <p>Prefer the VS Code clicks above. Use these only if you are comfortable in the terminal (second terminal tab if <code>npm run dev</code> is still running). Copy one command at a time.</p>
        <div class="command-grid command-grid-short">
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Create your task branch</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-branch">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-branch">git switch -c add-featured-project</pre></div>
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Check which files changed</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-status">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-status">git status</pre></div>
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Stage the changes you reviewed</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-add">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-add">git add src/data/profile.js</pre></div>
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Save a checkpoint</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-commit">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-commit">git commit -m "Add featured project card"</pre></div>
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Push the branch to GitHub</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-push">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-push">git push -u origin add-featured-project</pre></div>
        </div>
      </details>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="5"/> I created an issue, branched, opened a PR, got a partner +1 review, and merged (or followed the class checkpoint).</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-6" data-step="6">
    <div class="step-number">06</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 06 · PUBLISH THE SITE</div><h2>Deploy and verify the live site.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">Your starter ZIP already includes a GitHub Actions workflow. GitHub runs that file to build the site; GitHub Pages hosts the result. You do not write a workflow today — you read the one that shipped with the project, turn Pages on, and watch the run.</p>
      <div class="deploy-illustration"><article><span>01</span><b>Your repository</b><small>Astro source files</small></article><div>→</div><article><span>02</span><b>GitHub Actions</b><small>Install + build</small></article><div>→</div><article><span>03</span><b>GitHub Pages</b><small>Live website</small></article></div>

      <div class="plain-note"><b>Already in your ZIP</b><p>Open <code>.github/workflows/deploy.yml</code> in VS Code Explorer. That file came with <code>portfolio-starter.zip</code>. Later workshops go deeper on writing workflows; Workshop 1 uses this file as the example.</p></div>

      <figure class="explorer-figure" aria-label="Where to find the Actions workflow in VS Code">
        <div class="screen-label"><span>VS CODE · EXPLORER</span><span>THE WORKFLOW FILE</span></div>
        <div class="explorer-panel">
          <div class="explorer-sidebar" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="explorer-title">EXPLORER</div>
            <ul class="explorer-tree">
              <li class="folder open">my-resume-site</li>
              <li class="indent folder open">.github</li>
              <li class="indent2 folder open">workflows</li>
              <li class="indent3 file active">deploy.yml</li>
              <li class="indent folder">public</li>
              <li class="indent folder">src</li>
              <li class="indent file">package.json</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="explorer-copy">
            <p class="eyebrow">PATH TO OPEN</p>
            <p class="explorer-path"><code>.github</code> → <code>workflows</code> → <code>deploy.yml</code></p>
            <p>GitHub Actions reads this YAML file when a matching event happens. For this starter, a push to <strong>main</strong> starts the job.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">READ THIS · WHAT THE WORKFLOW DOES</span>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Trigger</strong> — <code>on: push</code> to the <code>main</code> branch (merging a pull request counts). Feature branches do not publish.</li>
          <li><strong>Build job</strong> — check out the repo, install Node 22, run <code>npm install</code> and <code>npm run build</code>, then upload the <code>dist/</code> folder.</li>
          <li><strong>Deploy job</strong> — publish that build to GitHub Pages.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">You do not need to edit <code>deploy.yml</code> in this workshop. If the Actions run fails, open the log and read the first clear error — often Pages was not set to “GitHub Actions” yet.</p>
      </div>

      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · PAGES SOURCE</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/configuring-a-publishing-source-for-your-github-pages-site" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pages/publishing-source-drop-down.png" alt="Real GitHub Pages settings showing the publishing source dropdown." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-pages.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real GitHub Pages UI. Under <strong>Build and deployment → Source</strong>, choose <strong>GitHub Actions</strong> for this starter.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · PAGES PUBLISHED URL</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/configuring-a-publishing-source-for-your-github-pages-site" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pages/click-pages-url-to-preview.png" alt="Real GitHub Pages settings and Visit site link for a published website." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-pages.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real GitHub Pages UI. Follow the published URL only after the latest build and deployment have succeeded.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB ACTIONS · JOB OUTPUT</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/monitor-workflows/use-workflow-run-logs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/repository/actions-quickstart-log-detail.png" alt="Real GitHub Actions log view with an expanded step and output." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-actions-run.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real Actions log view. Open the failed job, expand the failed step, and read the first actionable error before editing code.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · ENABLE PAGES AND VERIFY</span>
        <ol>
          <li>On GitHub, open your repository. Confirm you are looking at the <code>main</code> branch (branch dropdown near the file list).</li>
          <li>Click the <strong>Settings</strong> tab (repo settings, not your profile settings). You may need to scroll the tab bar on a small screen.</li>
          <li>In the left sidebar, click <strong>Pages</strong>.</li>
          <li>Under <strong>Build and deployment → Source</strong>, open the dropdown and choose <strong>GitHub Actions</strong>. Save if GitHub asks. Do this even if an Actions run already started.</li>
          <li>Click the <strong>Actions</strong> tab. Open the latest <strong>Build and deploy portfolio</strong> (or similarly named) workflow run.</li>
          <li>If the first run failed because Pages was not enabled yet: open that failed run → <strong>Re-run all jobs</strong>.</li>
          <li>Wait until the run shows green / success.</li>
          <li>Return to <strong>Settings → Pages</strong>. Click the published site URL (often <code>https://YOUR-USERNAME.github.io/YOUR-REPOSITORY/</code>).</li>
          <li>Check the homepage, your photo (if you added one), one project link, and a narrow browser window.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">The first deploy can take a few minutes. Feature branches do not publish this starter — merge to <code>main</code> first. If it fails, open the run, expand the failed step, and read the first clear error before changing files.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Understand a deployment error</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="debug-deployment">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="debug-deployment">Help me understand this build/deployment error in plain English: [paste the error text]. Tell me which step failed, the likely cause, and the smallest safe fix. Do not guess at settings that are not shown in the log.</pre></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="6"/> My published website opens, or I know exactly which deployment step needs help.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

